  {
   "id": "6JAP8pu9GNYJ",
   "from": "Martin Schultze",
   "author": "c3ac76bc87868f68",
   "date": "2014-09-04T09:37:08Z",
   "subject": "Re: Testing Assumptions for Mediationanalysis",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">To follow up on Sunthud&#39;s response: this approach does not work for manifest mediation models because the current version of <a href=\"https://github.com/yrosseel/lavaan/blob/master/R/lav_predict.R\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/yrosseel/lavaan/blob/master/R/lav_predict.R&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792431232828000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2SI2xd_TBnY4D6bncZ5iF-\">lav_predict</a> seems to be written specifically for factor scores. With manifest models it simply returns<br><br><font face=\"courier new, monospace\"><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#606\">Error</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> VETA</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">g</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">%*%</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> t</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">LAMBDA</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">g</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]])</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> non</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">conformable arguments</span></div></code></div><br></font><div><br></div><div><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\">So, using the example on the <a href=\"http://lavaan.ugent.be/tutorial/mediation.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://lavaan.ugent.be/tutorial/mediation.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792431232829000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0a1o3TVxXqFFG1Z6GDqYiz\">mediation tutorial page</a>, here&#39;s a (hacky) way of getting around this:</font></div><div><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br></font></div><div><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\">First, create the data:</font></div><div><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br></font></div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#008\">set</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">seed</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1234</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>X </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> rnorm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">100</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>M </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.5</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">X </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> rnorm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">100</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>Y </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.7</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">M </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> rnorm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">100</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#606\">Data</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">frame</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">X </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> X</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> Y </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> Y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> M </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> M</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><div style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif\"><br><br></div></div><div>Then define the variable as pseudo-latent (i.e. single-indicator latent variables with a loading of 1 and a residual of 0)</div><div><br></div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">latemod </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;<br>  lY =~ 1*Y<br>  Y ~~ 0*Y<br><br>  lM =~ 1*M<br>  M ~~ 0<br><br>  lX =~ 1*X<br>  X ~~ 0*X<br><br>  lM ~ lX<br>  lY ~ lX + lM&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>latefit </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sem</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">latemod</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div></code></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\"><br></font></div><div><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\">If you want, you can also define the manifest version to ensure that the results are the same:</font></div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">manimod </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;<br>  M ~ X<br>  Y ~ M + X&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>manifit </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sem</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">manimod</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><div><br></div>Using the predicted values of the pseudo-latent variables you can then run the diagnostics that Sunthud suggested.</div><div><br><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">fscores </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> predict</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">latefit</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div></code></div><br><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\"><br></font></div><div>On Tuesday, September 2, 2014 8:31:59 PM UTC+2, <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">laura.m...@googlemail.com</a> wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hi there!<div><br></div><div>Does anybody know if it is possible to test several assumptions (e.g. homoscedasticy, multikollinearity, normal distribution of residuals)</div><div>within a mediation model (with more than 1 mediator)?</div><div><br></div><div>Thank you so much for your advice!</div><div><br></div><div>Laura</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "QgktW1D5uJYJ",
   "from": "Sunthud Pornprasertmanit",
   "author": "af82fa98896529e2",
   "date": "2014-09-04T14:44:46Z",
   "subject": "Re: Testing Assumptions for Mediationanalysis",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Then, you may use the observed data instead of the factor scores:<div><br></div><div><div>set.seed(1234)</div><div>library(lavaan)</div><div>library(psych)</div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><br></div><div>X &lt;- rnorm(100)</div><div>\r\nM &lt;- 0.5*X + rnorm(100)</div><div>Y &lt;- 0.7*M + rnorm(100)</div><div>Data &lt;- data.frame(X = X, Y = Y, M = M)</div><div><br></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div>manimod &lt;- &#39;</div><div>  M ~ a*X</div><div>  Y ~ b*M + c*X&#39;</div><div>\r\nmanifit &lt;- sem(manimod,Data)</div><div><br></div><div>describe(Data)</div><div><br></div><div>predM &lt;- coef(manifit)[&quot;a&quot;] * Data$X</div><div>predY &lt;- coef(manifit)[&quot;c&quot;] * Data$X + coef(manifit)[&quot;b&quot;] * Data$M</div>\r\n<div>residM &lt;- Data$M - predM</div><div>residY &lt;- Data$Y - predY</div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><br></div><div># Residual plot to check the heteroscadasticity</div><div>plot(predM, residM)</div><div>plot(predY, residY)</div><div><br></div>\r\n<div># Check the skewness and kurtosis of residuals</div><div>describe(cbind(residM, residY))</div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Thu, Sep 4, 2014 at 4:37 AM, Martin Schultze <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">marts...@googlemail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br>\r\n</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">To follow up on Sunthud&#39;s response: this approach does not work for manifest mediation models because the current version of <a href=\"https://github.com/yrosseel/lavaan/blob/master/R/lav_predict.R\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/yrosseel/lavaan/blob/master/R/lav_predict.R&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792431232833000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2QPtx7FFyS4-ZeWLpBWvia\">lav_predict</a> seems to be written specifically for factor scores. With manifest models it simply returns<br>\r\n<br><font face=\"courier new, monospace\"><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#606\">Error</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> VETA</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">g</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">%*%</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> t</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">LAMBDA</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">g</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]])</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> non</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">conformable arguments</span></div>\r\n</code></div><br></font><div><br></div><div><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\">So, using the example on the <a href=\"http://lavaan.ugent.be/tutorial/mediation.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://lavaan.ugent.be/tutorial/mediation.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792431232833000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1XbNQovK4RHKcKVVFq-rvF\">mediation tutorial page</a>, here&#39;s a (hacky) way of getting around this:</font></div>\r\n<div><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br></font></div><div><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\">First, create the data:</font></div><div><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br></font></div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\">\r\n<code><div><span style=\"color:#008\">set</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">seed</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1234</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>\r\nX </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> rnorm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">100</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>M </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.5</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">X </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> rnorm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">100</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>\r\nY </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.7</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">M </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> rnorm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">100</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>\r\n</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Data</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">frame</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">X </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> X</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> Y </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> Y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> M </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> M</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>\r\n</span></div></code></div><div style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif\"><br><br></div></div><div>Then define the variable as pseudo-latent (i.e. single-indicator latent variables with a loading of 1 and a residual of 0)</div>\r\n<div><br></div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">latemod </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;<br>\r\n  lY =~ 1*Y<br>  Y ~~ 0*Y<br><br>  lM =~ 1*M<br>  M ~~ 0<br><br>  lX =~ 1*X<br>  X ~~ 0*X<br><br>  lM ~ lX<br>  lY ~ lX + lM&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>latefit </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sem</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">latemod</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div>\r\n</code></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\"><br></font></div><div><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\">If you want, you can also define the manifest version to ensure that the results are the same:</font></div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\">\r\n<code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">manimod </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;<br>  M ~ X<br>  Y ~ M + X&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>manifit </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sem</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">manimod</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>\r\n</span></div></code></div><div><br></div>Using the predicted values of the pseudo-latent variables you can then run the diagnostics that Sunthud suggested.</div><div><br><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\">\r\n<code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">fscores </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> predict</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">latefit</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div>\r\n</code></div><br><br></div><div><div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\"><br></font></div><div>On Tuesday, September 2, 2014 8:31:59 PM UTC+2, <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">laura.m...@googlemail.com</a> wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">\r\n<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi there!<div><br></div><div>Does anybody know if it is possible to test several assumptions (e.g. homoscedasticy, multikollinearity, normal distribution of residuals)</div><div>within a mediation model (with more than 1 mediator)?</div>\r\n<div><br></div><div>Thank you so much for your advice!</div><div><br></div><div>Laura</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div></div></blockquote></div></div></div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ],
